WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.520
A ganglion cell in a rabbit retina fires hard when a spot moves one way across its

2
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.020
field. It hardly fires when the spot moves the other way.

3
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.000
The first way is its preferred direction.

4
00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:18.580
The opposite way is the null direction.

5
00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:23.160
Where in the retina is that difference computed, and by which cells?

6
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:28.930
Barlow and Levick recorded from such cells in the living rabbit.

7
00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:35.230
They flashed two neighbouring places in one receptive field, one after the other.

8
00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:40.770
In the preferred order, the pair gave about the sum of the two single responses.

9
00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:45.210
In the null order, it gave far fewer spikes than that sum.

10
00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:50.770
They inferred that small subunits suppress sequences running the null way.

11
00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.170
They were tentative about which cells do this.

12
00:00:55.033 --> 00:00:58.553
Later work turned to the starburst amacrine cell.

13
00:00:59.313 --> 00:01:03.493
Its dendrites radiate from the cell body like the spokes of a wheel.

14
00:01:04.773 --> 00:01:11.913
Euler, Detwiler and Denk imaged calcium in single dendrites of ON starburst cells in

15
00:01:11.913 --> 00:01:13.253
isolated rabbit retina.

16
00:01:14.373 --> 00:01:19.433
A dendrite gave larger calcium signals to motion outward from the cell body than

17
00:01:19.433 --> 00:01:23.753
inward. The voltage at the cell body did not show that preference.

18
00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:30.827
So each branch has its own preferred direction, set by where the branch points.

19
00:01:32.047 --> 00:01:37.007
Briggman, Helmstaedter and Denk then tested the wiring in mouse retina.

20
00:01:37.907 --> 00:01:43.647
They imaged each ganglion cell's preferred direction, then reconstructed the same

21
00:01:43.647 --> 00:01:45.907
tissue by electron microscopy.

22
00:01:46.827 --> 00:01:52.347
Most starburst input came from branches pointing roughly along the ganglion cell's

23
00:01:52.347 --> 00:01:58.847
null direction. Branch direction predicted connection better than the line between cell bodies.

25
00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.850
How does that asymmetry arise?

26
00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.590
Pruning would delete the preferred side connections.

27
00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:12.070
Wei, Feller and colleagues recorded pairs of starburst and ganglion cells in

28
00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:13.210
developing mouse retina.

29
00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:19.230
At postnatal days 4 and 7, inhibition was similar from both sides.

30
00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:22.850
Later, inhibition from the null side grew.

31
00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:28.090
Inhibition from the preferred side stayed about the same, and those connections were

32
00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:29.290
still there in adults.

33
00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:35.643
Removing the whole starburst population from adult mice abolished direction

34
00:02:35.643 --> 00:02:37.703
selectivity in the ganglion cells.

35
00:02:38.263 --> 00:02:40.783
That shows the cells are needed in that assay.

36
00:02:41.463 --> 00:02:45.803
It does not say whether acetylcholine or GABA carries the asymmetry.

37
00:02:46.683 --> 00:02:47.763
So keep this rule.

38
00:02:48.723 --> 00:02:54.223
Direction is computed branch by branch, and inhibition strengthens on the null side.
